1. Resolve location
2. Build absolute path
3. Normalize path (remove ../)
4. stat()
5. If regular file
6. Extract extension
7. Check cgiMap
8. Execute

e.g.
if a url /abc/xyz.php matches a location /abc/ and abc is not cgi-enabled, would this location just return /abc/index.html?

No - it would match on longest path first, and then if that location is cgi-enabled, then it would run cgi
   - if not cgi-enabled, it would treat the request as a static-file request
   - index.html is only returned when request is for a directory (ends /), not for a request for a file 

location / {
    cgi_path /usr/bin/php-cgi .php;
}

location /abc/ {
    root /var/www/abc;
}

1. Longest prefix match → location /abc/
2. Build filesystem path → /var/www/abc/xyz.php
3. stat() that file
4. If regular file → serve it as static
5. If not found → 404